WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(cheerful music)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.740
- [Narrator] In order to avoid,

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:04.500
- An economic and financial catastrophe.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.990
- [Narrator] by defaulting,
Democrats and Republicans

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.200
have to agree to raise the
debt ceiling by early June.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.450
The negotiations are ongoing.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.280
- There's no agreement.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.750
We're still apart.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.400
- I've done my part.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.780
- [Narrator] But what
if there were options

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.120
for the White House to go around Congress

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.750
to avoid a default even if
they're not better ones?

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.870
- If Congress does not
address the debt limit

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.265
there are no good options

00:00:29.265 --> 00:00:32.670
the Treasury or the government can use

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.710
to save us from catastrophe.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.890
- Any deviations from that
path would be highly risky.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.230
- [Narrator] Highly risky
and highly unlikely,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.690
but some are really being considered.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:45.150
- I think it is worth taking seriously.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:46.440
You know, they're all bad,

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.340
but how they compare to
the badness of default.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.650
- [Narrator] Here are the
three risky, unlikely,

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.510
but not improbable paths

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.360
Biden could take to avoid a default.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.900
Let's start with the first
one, the trillion dollar coin.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.300
A bill in the 90s aimed
at making it easier

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.570
to mint commemorative
coins technically does say

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.340
that the Treasury Secretary can mint

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.820
and issue platinum coins
in any denomination.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.270
So the thought is, why not
mint one for 1 trillion

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.993
and then have enough money to
not need to take on more debt?

00:01:17.993 --> 00:01:21.540
- I think the platinum
coin is the most unlikely.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.310
- [Narrator] Jason Furman
was President Obama's

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.440
top economist and was at the White House

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.810
during the first big debt ceiling crisis.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.350
- I remember in 2011, I
read about the platinum coin

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.660
on a blog post someone sent me.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.015
I went to Tim Geithner, who
was then Treasury Secretary

00:01:36.015 --> 00:01:38.137
just in the hallway
before a meeting and said,

00:01:38.137 --> 00:01:40.110
"Hey, what do you think
of the platinum coin?"

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.850
He's like, "The platinum what?"

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.320
And I'm like, "Oh, the platinum coin.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.180
We can mint a platinum coin."

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:46.980
He's like, "Yeah, what
are you talking about?

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.030
That's crazy."

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:51.210
- [Narrator] The current Treasury
Secretary feels similarly.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.220
- It's really a gimmick.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.410
- [Narrator] The Treasury
also can't just mint the coin

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.630
and automatically start spending it.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:00.030
- They would need money
in what is essentially

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.240
their checking account which
is held at the Federal Reserve.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.650
And so you would need them

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:08.190
to bring that trillion dollar
coin to the Federal Reserve

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.320
the Federal Reserve to accept it

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.830
and then credit the
government's checking account

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:14.970
with a trillion dollars.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.610
And then the federal government
could use that money.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:18.443
- [Narrator] But it's not clear

00:02:18.443 --> 00:02:20.790
the Federal Reserve would
even accept the coin.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.650
- There are no rabbits to
be pulled out of hats here.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:23.483
This only ends with Congress.

00:02:23.483 --> 00:02:25.050
- I know you don't like yes or nos,

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.780
but if you were sent
a trillion dollar coin

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.900
and asked to treat it
as a trillion dollars,

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.900
will you treat it as a trillion dollars?

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.653
- That would be a rabbit
coming out of a hat.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.680
- [Narrator] The second but
slightly more likely option

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.470
is the treasury issuing premium bonds.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.120
The way the treasury takes on debt

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.880
in the first place is by selling bonds.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.090
A $1,000, 10 year bond
with a 3% interest rate,

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.830
also called a coupon rate,

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.860
would pay $30 every year for 10 years.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.440
So it's really worth $1,300.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.000
By the way, these interest payments

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.840
are one of many government
expenses that are due regularly,

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.360
and if the Treasury doesn't
have enough money to pay them

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.310
like they say, they
won't if the debt ceiling

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:12.570
is raised by the X date around
June 1st, that's a default.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:16.170
- The world has never doubted that America

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:20.070
will pay the principle
and interest on its bonds.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.250
A default would crack open the foundations

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.910
upon which our financial system is built.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.680
- [Narrator] The treasury
auctions off bonds

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.460
and maybe gets $1,100
for that $1,000 bond.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:36.000
They've brought in an
extra a $100 in revenue.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.320
When the debt ceiling is hit,

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.210
they aren't allowed to take on new debt

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:43.890
by issuing new bonds, but
they could reissue bonds.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.290
If an old $1,000 bond's term is ending,

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.510
they could put that bond
back up for auction.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.310
It's not new debt,

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:55.290
but they could reissue with a
higher coupon rate, say 10%.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:59.400
Then the bond would be
worth $2,000 over 10 years.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.230
It could bring in more money at auction

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:02.370
to fund the government

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:05.520
without technically taking out new debt.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.430
- Even the proponents of
premium bonds would admit

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:12.000
that they are a bad idea compared
to raising the debt limit.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.520
Launching a brand new type of bond

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.010
that pays a very high coupon payment

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.470
so it has a lower face value

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.740
in the middle of a
financially turbulent period

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:27.740
would be a real recipe for a
lot of financial market chaos.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.960
Now, you do need to compare that chaos

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.930
to the chaos you would get
if you went past the X date

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:36.330
and started not paying your obligations.

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.640
So this becomes a, you know,

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.460
comparison of different forms of poison.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.440
- [Narrator] The third
and most likely option,

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.540
- You could call it the most likely option

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.510
but I'd rather call it
the least unlikely option.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.580
- [Narrator] Okay, the
least unlikely option,

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.920
would be to use the 14th Amendment.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:54.907
Article 1 of the Constitution says,

00:04:54.907 --> 00:04:58.980
"Congress has the power to
pay the debts of the US.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:00.607
But the 14th Amendment says,

00:05:00.607 --> 00:05:04.080
"The validity of the public
debt shall not be questioned,"

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.760
which could be interpreted to mean

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.670
the debt limit itself is unconstitutional.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:09.930
- Say, you know what?

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:11.700
We can issue whatever debt we want.

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.160
We're not gonna invent
some brand new coin.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.740
We're not gonna invent some
brand new type of debt.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:20.190
We're just gonna go ahead
and continue to issue debt.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.390
- [Narrator] This is
something the White House

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:23.220
is really looking into.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:24.867
- I've been considering
the 14th Amendment.

00:05:24.867 --> 00:05:28.020
The problem is it would
have to be litigated.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.540
- If you did Invoke, the 14th Amendment,

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.160
and financial markets thought

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.770
you had a good enough
chance of prevailing,

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:37.200
they would continue to lend you money.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.080
And so, you'd be buying yourself time.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.730
It might be that the courts

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:44.280
supported the theory
of the 14th Amendment,

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.200
the problem is you may
not get that outcome.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:47.820
You may get the opposite,

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.640
and then you're back to the drawing board

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.880
with having created a bunch of chaos.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:55.410
- [Narrator] But even
if the 14th Amendment

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:56.490
isn't invoked now,

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.220
it might be for future debt limit battles.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:00.390
- I'll be very blunt with you.

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:01.290
When we get by this,

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:04.230
I'm thinking about taking a look at

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:05.970
months down the road as to see whether

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:07.290
what the court would say about

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:10.590
whether or not it does work.

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:12.870
- [Narrator] Also, one
path House Democrats

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:14.580
not the White House could take

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.370
is something called a discharge petition.

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.650
Basically, if Speaker McCarthy refuses

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:22.830
to bring a clean debt ceiling
raise to the House floor,

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:24.510
a majority of members could bring it

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:27.030
to the floor after a lengthy process.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:29.070
It's in the works but many experts say

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:30.900
this isn't highly likely either.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:33.870
- Discharge petitions rarely succeed,

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:35.940
and when they do, working
through the process

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:37.740
can take more than a month.

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:41.130
And we do not have more than
a month to resolve this issue.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.390
- [Narrator] Which is why those in charge

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:44.460
of the country's financial system say,

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:46.200
- There's only one way forward here,

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.240
and that is for Congress
to raise the debt ceiling

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:49.740
so that the United States government

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:52.140
can pay all of its obligations when due.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:55.200
- The only good outcome is one in which

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.849
Congress asks to raise the debt ceiling.

00:06:59.849 --> 00:07:02.599
(cheerful music)

